  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B006",
        "laureates": [
          "Immanuel Bloch",
          "Markus Greiner",
          "Peter Zoller"
        ],
        "citation": "For establishing ultracold atoms in optical lattices as a controlled platform for quantum many-body physics."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B002",
    "B004"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Optical lattices made quantum many-body systems accessible to controlled preparation and measurement, opening a new experimental route to phenomena that are difficult to isolate in solids. The platform and its results have been reproduced and extended over decades. This is a mature experimental achievement whose significance does not depend on whether it ultimately becomes a predictive simulator for real materials.",
    "case_against": "The achievement spans theoretical foundations and multiple experimental milestones, and the proposed three names cannot capture the broader effort. Cirac and other groups may merit consideration depending on where the discovery is bounded. Some applications of the platform remain developing.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson notes that Cirac and other experimental groups may also warrant recognition, depending on how the discovery is framed.",
        "response": "That is a serious attribution question. I would frame the prize around the controlled optical-lattice many-body platform and its defining experimental demonstrations, with Zoller representing the theoretical vision. The historical record should establish whether this scope supports the proposed names and how Cirac’s contributions fit; the shortlist’s fixed credited names do not remove that need."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "pearce-mark",
        "point": "Pearce questions whether the platform’s eventual reach as a predictive simulator for difficult systems is sufficiently established.",
        "response": "I agree that predictive power remains an open measure of its future impact. But the awardable achievement can be the experimentally verified creation of a controllable many-body system and the new phenomena it makes accessible. Its scientific value is already demonstrated without requiring a claim that it has solved the problem of simulating real materials."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The historical boundary between the foundational theory and defining experimental milestones, including the roles of Cirac and other groups, needs careful review."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
